    "last_assistant_text": "The PoC triggered successfully. The ASAN output confirms the exact vulnerability:\n\n- `heap-buffer-overflow on address ... WRITE of size 4` at `Hex::writeUInt16`\n- Called from `Utils::escapeUtf8Char` (line 208) → `Utils::escapeString` (line 292) → `Serializer::serializeString` (line 34)\n- The 111-byte region is the buffer allocated by `String(escapedSize)` where `escapedSize = 110` (but ASAN shows 111 because of the null terminator)\n- The overflow was detected because the accumulated offset from 10 iterations (each writing 12 instead of 11 bytes) eventually writes past the allocation\n\nThe earlier attempts with fewer sequences didn't trigger because the 1-byte overflow was within `std::string`'s internal padding or null terminator space.\n\nTriggered: true, exit code: 134 (SIGABRT from ASAN).\n\nThe PoC is `poc5.bin` — a JSON object `{\"s\":\"<10 copies of 0xF8 0x80 0x90 0x80 0x80>\"}` that deserializes a string containing ten 5-byte non-standard UTF-8 sequences. During re-serialization, `calcEscapedStringSize` allocates 11 bytes per sequence (110 total), but `escapeUtf8Char` writes 12 bytes per sequence (120 total), overflowing by 10 bytes.",
    "submission_command_calls": 5,
    "final_message": "The PoC triggered successfully. The ASAN output confirms the exact vulnerability:\n\n- `heap-buffer-overflow on address ... WRITE of size 4` at `Hex::writeUInt16`\n- Called from `Utils::escapeUtf8Char` (line 208) → `Utils::escapeString` (line 292) → `Serializer::serializeString` (line 34)\n- The 111-byte region is the buffer allocated by `String(escapedSize)` where `escapedSize = 110` (but ASAN shows 111 because of the null terminator)\n- The overflow was detected because the accumulated offset from 10 iterations (each writing 12 instead of 11 bytes) eventually writes past the allocation\n\nThe earlier attempts with fewer sequences didn't trigger because the 1-byte overflow was within `std::string`'s internal padding or null terminator space.\n\nTriggered: true, exit code: 134 (SIGABRT from ASAN).\n\nThe PoC is `poc5.bin` — a JSON object `{\"s\":\"<10 copies of 0xF8 0x80 0x90 0x80 0x80>\"}` that deserializes a string containing ten 5-byte non-standard UTF-8 sequences. During re-serialization, `calcEscapedStringSize` allocates 11 bytes per sequence (110 total), but `escapeUtf8Char` writes 12 bytes per sequence (120 total), overflowing by 10 bytes.",
